# 5NF database design

When adding or changing tables, columns, FKs, or domain models in this repo, design for **fifth normal form (5NF / PJ/NF)** unless the user explicitly waives it.

## Do

- One clear **predicate** per table (what one row means).
- Decompose join dependencies: no packing unrelated facts into one wide row.
- Prefer subtype / detail tables over nullable “sometimes filled” columns (e.g. milk fields → `milk_job_details`).
- Prefer parties / contacts as their own relvar when reused across facts.
- Keep association tables as true M:N pivots with composite keys.
- Document controlled exceptions (snapshots, event logs, external geocode caches).

## Do not

- Add denormalized copies “for convenience” without naming the exception and why.
- Put request-level and hop-level facts on the same row when they join-depend separately.
- Store the same odometer/event fact in two places (single source of truth).

## Project reference

Read and follow [`docs/technical/5nf-target-schema.md`](docs/technical/5nf-target-schema.md) for Plasma’s landed shape and allowed exceptions (e.g. `content_label_snapshot`).

For definitions and industry practice, see [`.cursor/skills/5nf-database-design/references.md`](.cursor/skills/5nf-database-design/references.md) (Fagin PJ/NF, Silberschatz, Date, Aalto, Postgres cookbook).

## API boundary

5NF is a **storage** rule. Keep existing camelCase JSON contracts unless the user asks to change the API; compose the flat resource from normalized tables in services/resources.
